✅ Step 1: Start Minikube
Command
minikube start

Expected Output (sample)
😄  minikube v1.34.0 on Ubuntu 20.04
✨  Using docker driver
👍  Starting control plane node minikube in cluster minikube
🏄  Done! Kubectl is now configured to use "minikube" cluster.

✅ Step 2: Create Deployment for Nginx
Command
kubectl create deployment nginx-deploy --image=nginx

Expected Output
deployment.apps/nginx-deploy created

(Optional for exam) Verify deployment:
kubectl get deployments


Expected:

NAME            READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE   AGE
nginx-deploy    1/1     1            1           10s

✅ Step 3: Expose Deployment as Service on port 8090
Command
kubectl expose deployment nginx-deploy --type=NodePort --port=80

Expected Output
service/nginx-deploy exposed


Now we get the NodePort assigned:

kubectl get svc

Expected Output
NAME            TYPE        CLUSTER-IP      EXTERNAL-IP   PORT(S)        AGE
nginx-deploy    NodePort    10.96.54.120    <none>        80:3XXXX/TCP   30s


PORT → 3XXXX is the NodePort value.

✅ Step 4: Map NodePort to host port 8090 (Minikube command)

This exposes service directly on localhost:8090

Command
minikube service nginx-deploy --url

Expected Output
http://127.0.0.1:8090


⚠️ In the exam, if it gives another port like http://127.0.0.1:30080,
You run:

minikube service nginx-deploy --url | sed 's/[0-9]\+$/8090/'


But safest: simply note the URL shown and open it.

✅ Step 5: Verify Nginx in Browser (localhost:8090)

👉 Open:

http://localhost:8090

Expected

Nginx default welcome page:

Welcome to nginx!


(Take screenshot for proof.)

